---

<CaseStudy
  name="Tutor-set pricing"
  tagline="Enabling Dojo Tutor’s tutors to set their own prices and take control of their businesses."
  noindex
  details={[
    { label: 'Responsibilities', items: ['Product design', 'User research', 'Product building'] },
    { label: 'Collaborators', items: ['1 product manager', '4 engineers'] },
    { label: 'Platform', items: ['Web'] },
    { label: 'Year', items: ['2026'] },
  ]}
>
  <Fragment slot="headline">
    Enabling Dojo Tutor’s tutors to <em>set their own prices</em> and take control of their businesses.
  </Fragment>

  <p class="callout" slot="meta">
    70% of tutors opted in within 3 months, enabling Dojo Tutor to perform experiments with pricing.
  </p>

  <div class="intro">
    <p>
      <a class="text-link" href="/dojo-tutor">Dojo Tutor</a> is ClassDojo’s marketplace connecting tutors and families.
      Prices used to be fixed by the platform across all grades, subjects, and student needs. We wanted tutors to set
      their own prices so they could charge more for specialty topics and open up more affordable options for families.
      I led the design and research, and built much of the frontend.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Figure images={[{ name: 'dojo-pricing-hero', alt: 'Setting a group class price: recommended range, take-home pay after the platform fee, and Earnings Boost' }]} eager />

  <div class="section-head">
    <h2>The stakes</h2>
    <p>
      Dojo Tutor set every price and pay rate centrally for all subjects, grades, and students. This constrained supply
      as many tutors saw our fixed pricing as too low, especially for higher grades and upmarket topics such as
      standardized test prep. This also prevented tutors in lower cost-of-living regions to offer less expensive classes
      and compete on price.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head">
    <h2>Tutors feared a race to the bottom</h2>
    <p>
      I ran surveys and user interviews and discovered that most tutors wanted to set their own prices, but many worried
      about a “race to the bottom” as tutors competed on price.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tutors also feared that families would not understand why different tutors would charge different prices and pick
      tutors completely based on price.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head">
    <h2>Establishing a fee structure</h2>
    <p>Along with product, finance and operations counterparts I explored different variables for tutor-set pricing:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Adjusting take rate.</strong> Exploring different fee amounts for charging tutors.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Loyalty incentives.</strong> Rewarding tutors for retaining subscribers by increasing their take rate after
        a set number of classes with a family.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Giving tutors control over discounts.</strong> Discounts were previously set at the platform level. Tutors
        could now decide whether to accept the tradeoffs of offering discounts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The rollout.</strong> Deciding whether to make the release opt-in or opt-out as well as permanent vs
        reversible.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Given the potential impact on margin and overall revenue, this project had high visibility with leadership. My
      product and ops counterparts and I walked leadership through different combinations of options until we reached a
      consensus.
    </p>
    <p>
      We landed on opt-in, tutor-set pricing with guardrails: marketplace fees calculated to increase tutor take-home
      pay, recommended ranges based on market data, and tools that help tutors communicate their value instead of
      competing as an undifferentiated listing.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head">
    <h2>What we shipped</h2>
    <p>
      I designed and built much of the frontend for tutor pricing, including the first-time experience, setting rates,
      adding discounts, and surfacing market data so tutors can price competitively.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Figure
    images={[
      { name: 'dojo-pricing-earnings', alt: 'Estimated earnings increase from the new pricing model' },
      { name: 'dojo-pricing-setup', alt: 'Setting 1:1 tutoring prices during onboarding' },
    ]}
    lead="Opting in."
  >
    Tutors see what they stand to earn before switching, then set prices with recommendations based on similar
    classes. Leading with take-home pay showed tutors that they stood to gain additional take-home pay from the new
    pricing model.
  </Figure>

  <Figure
    images={[{ name: 'dojo-class-prices', alt: 'Editing a class price with the recommended range, take-home pay, and Earnings Boost' }]}
    lead="Pricing with guardrails."
  >
    Market-based recommended ranges, take-home pay after fees, and the Earnings Boost for long-term family
    relationships.
  </Figure>

  <div class="section-head">
    <h2>Results</h2>
    <p>
      Despite widespread apprehension from our tutor community, over 70% of tutors opted into the new pricing model
      within the first 3 months of release.
    </p>
    <p>
      The majority of tutors accepted platform recommendations and we saw an expected spread of tutors offering both
      higher and lower prices.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="section-head">
    <h2>What I’d do differently</h2>
    <p>
      Show external stakeholders the different pricing options in end-to-end prototypes that showed the impact to both
      tutors and families. The different pricing options were too abstract to understand the impact on end users.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p class="back">
    <a class="more" href="/dojo-tutor">‹ Back to Dojo Tutor</a>
  </p>
</CaseStudy>

